#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# rgb_color

#% text
# `rgb_color(r, g, b)` is the colour with red, green and blue components r, g, b between 0 and 1.

#% text
# `rgb_color(r, g, b, a)` is the colour with opacity a.

#% text
# `rgb_color("#rrggbb")` is the colour of a hexadecimal code.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# In graphics a colour is a directive: what follows it is drawn in that colour.

#% item
# Components outside [0, 1] are clipped when the graphic is drawn.

#% item
# A hexadecimal code becomes the three components as machine reals.

#% item
# The named colours (`red`, `green`, `blue`, `black`, `white`, `gray`, `light_gray`, `dark_gray`, `cyan`, `magenta`, `yellow`, `orange`, `purple`, `brown`, `pink`) can be used wherever a colour is expected.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A colour stays as written:

#% cell
rgb_color(0.2, 0.4, 0.8)
#% output
#= rgb_color(0.2, 0.4, 0.8)

#% text
# A hexadecimal code:

#% cell
rgb_color("#ff8000")
#% output
#= rgb_color(1.0, 0.5019607843137255, 0.0)

#% text
# Colours as directives:

#% cell
graphics([rgb_color(0.2, 0.4, 0.8), disk(), rgb_color(1, 1, 1, 0.5), disk([0.3, 0.3], 0.5)])
#% output
#= image("rgb_color.files/rgb_color-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same colour by hue, saturation and brightness:

#% cell
color_convert(rgb_color(0.2, 0.4, 0.8), hue)
#% output
#= hue(0.611111111111111, 0.7500000000000001, 0.8)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Components must be numbers (or symbols that become numbers later):

#% cell
rgb_color(1, "half", 0)
#% output
#! failure(argument, rgb_color, 2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hue`, `gray_level`, `opacity`, `color_convert`, `blend`
